            <ArticleTitle>The Traumatized TFCC: An Illustrated Review of the Anatomy and Injury Patterns of the Triangular Fibrocartilage Complex.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>39-50</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1067/j.cpradiol.2015.05.004</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0363-0188(15)00084-5</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The triangular fibrocartilage complex (TFCC) plays an important role in wrist biomechanics and is prone to traumatic and degenerative injury, making it a common source of ulnar-sided wrist pain. Because of this, the TFCC is frequently imaged, and a detailed understanding of its anatomy and injury patterns is critical in generating an accurate report to help guide treatment. In this review, we provide a detailed overview of TFCC anatomy, its normal appearance on magnetic resonance imaging, the spectrum of TFCC injuries based on the Palmer classification system, and pitfalls in accurate assessment.</AbstractText>
